<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumbo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumbo is a town in the North-West Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and the capital of the Nso kingdom, whose Fon has his palace there. It became the seat of Nso after several moves of the capital and was occupied by German forces in the first years of the 20th century. Kumbo is now one of the largest towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon-grassfields">Cameroon Grassfields</a> and a Catholic diocesan seat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumbo.jpg" alt="Kumbo" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tavmjong · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbo_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kumbo, also known as Kimbo, lies about 2,000 m above sea level on the Bamenda Highlands Ring Road, some 110 km from Bamenda, and is the capital of Bui Division. It is spread over three hilly settlements, Tobin, Mbveh and Squares, and had 80,212 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2005. Lamnso is the common language, beside Pidgin, English, Oku, Djottin, Fulfulde, French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>. The Mbve market sells goods from the region, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Fon's palace, seat of the paramount ruler of the Nso people, is the centre of the town's social and cultural life: carved wooden buildings around two courtyards where the Fon receives his people and advisers, and a statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngonnso">Ngonso</a>, held to be the first queen of the Nso. Beside it stand the houses of the Ngwerong and Nggiri societies, which help the Fon in his task. The Missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-heart">Sacred Heart</a> arrived in 1912 and founded their mission in 1913, and most of the Nso are Christians.</p><p>The stone cathedral, built in the 1950s on a hilltop that dominates the town, became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> when the diocese of Kumbo was created. In May and June 2021, during the Anglophone Crisis, the Cameroonian Army carried out Operation Bui Clean to drive Ambazonian separatists from the town and its surroundings, with heavy casualties on both sides, and insurgents attacked the town again in April 2024.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kumbo is the second-largest town of the North West and is known for its horse racing, a custom brought by the Mbororo cattle herders, whose annual race is held at the Tobin Municipal Stadium. The Musa Heritage Gallery, founded in 1996, keeps over 400 carved masks, statues and other works of the Western Grassfields. The statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngonnso">Ngonnso</a>, founder of the kingdom, is held in a museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and people in Nso have called for its return.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumbo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early capital moved several times before settling at <b>Kumbo</b>, and Nso expanded by absorbing neighbouring chiefdoms in the 18th and 19th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-nso">Kingdom of Nso</a></li></ul></section>
